.TH "MAW-FORMATS" "5" "" "maw 0.1.1" "maw manual"
.SH NAME
maw-formats \- every output format and how nix values map to it
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Set with \fBformat = "<name>"\fR in \fBlib.program\fR, or call the generator directly (\fBlib.toCSS { ... }\fR). Every format accepts \fBlib.raw\fR at any node.
.PP
Floats print in their shortest form (\fB0.5\fR). When you build a string yourself, use \fBbuiltins.toJSON 0.5\fR rather than \fBtoString 0.5\fR, which gives \fB0.500000\fR.
.SH "INI (LIB.TOINI)"
.PP
Attrsets become \fB[sections]\fR. Scalars at the top level are written first, without a section. Lists repeat the key. A \fBlib.raw\fR in a section's place is written verbatim, with no header.
.PP
.EX
{ shell = "bash"; main.term = "xterm-256color"; }
.EE
.PP
.EX
shell=bash

[main]
term=xterm-256color
.EE
.SH "KEYVALUE (LIB.TOKEYVALUE)"
.PP
\fBkey=value\fR lines. Lists repeat the key.
.SH "JSON (LIB.TOJSON)"
.PP
Indented JSON. A \fBlib.raw\fR value is spliced in as literal JSON.
.SH "KDL (LIB.TOKDL)"
.PP
For niri. The shape of each value decides the node:
.PP
.EX
Nix                                                      KDL
gaps = 16;                                               gaps 16
spawn = [ "foot" "-e" "htop" ];                          spawn "foot" "-e" "htop"
layout = { gaps = 16; };                                 layout { gaps 16 }
prefer-no-csd = null;                                    prefer-no-csd
spawn-at-startup = [ [ "waybar" ] [ "mako" ] ];          the node twice, once per list
window-rule = [ { ... } { ... } ];                       the block twice, in list order
output = lib.kdl.node [ "eDP-1" ] { } { scale = 1.5; };  output "eDP-1" { scale 1.5 }
.EE
.PP
\fBlib.kdl.node args props children\fR covers anything else, like binds with properties:
.PP
.EX
"Mod+Shift+Slash" = lib.kdl.node [ ] { hotkey-overlay-title = "help"; } { show-hotkey-overlay = null; };
.EE
.PP
A node repeated with one argument each needs a list of lists, since a flat list is read as several arguments:
.PP
.EX
preset-column-widths.proportion = map (width: [ width ]) [ 0.25 0.5 0.75 ];
.EE
.PP
For property-only nodes like \fBmatch app-id="firefox"\fR, a small helper keeps modules readable:
.PP
.EX
props = attrs: lib.kdl.node [ ] attrs { };
window-rule = [ { match = props { app-id = "firefox"; }; open-maximized = true; } ];
.EE
.PP
A \fBlib.raw\fR in a node's place is written verbatim, and isn't re-indented.
.SH "CSS (LIB.TOCSS)"
.PP
Selectors map to properties. Nested attrsets are nested selectors: plain keys become descendants, and \fB&\fR stands for the parent. Keys starting with \fB@\fR that hold attrsets become blocks. Scalars at rule level become statements, and statements are written before rules.
.PP
.EX
{
  "@define-color bg" = "#1e1e2e";
  "*".font-family = [ "\e"JetBrains Mono\e"" "monospace" ];
  "window#waybar" = {
    background = "@bg";
    "#clock".padding = "0 8px";
    "&.hidden".opacity = 0;
  };
  "@media (max-width: 800px)"."#clock".font-size = "10px";
}
.EE
.PP
.EX
@define-color bg #1e1e2e;

* {
  font-family: "JetBrains Mono", monospace;
}

@media (max-width: 800px) {
  #clock {
    font-size: 10px;
  }
}

window#waybar {
  background: @bg;
}

window#waybar #clock {
  padding: 0 8px;
}

window#waybar.hidden {
  opacity: 0;
}
.EE
.PP
When cascade order matters, pass a list of rule sets instead of one attrset. Each set is rendered in turn:
.PP
.EX
[
  { "#clock, #cpu".color = "#bababf"; }
  { "#clock".color = "#f2f2f7"; }   # must come after the group rule
]
.EE
.PP
A key can hold several selectors (\fB"#clock, #cpu"\fR), so a helper can share properties:
.PP
.EX
each = selectors: properties: { ${lib.concatStringsSep ",\en" selectors} = properties; };
.EE
.PP
Property lists are joined with \fB, \fR. Values are written as-is, so quote font names yourself when they need it. Nesting under a comma selector (\fB"a, b"\fR) isn't expanded per selector; write those rules out flat.
.SH "SHELL (LIB.TOSHELL)"
.PP
For bashrc-style files. Takes \fB{ aliases; exports; extra; }\fR:
.PP
.EX
{
  aliases.ll = "ls -l";
  exports.EDITOR = "nvim";
  exports.PATH = lib.raw ''"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"'';
  extra = lib.raw "PS1='\e\ew \e\e$ '\en";
}
.EE
.PP
Values are single-quoted. Use \fBlib.raw\fR to keep \fB$\fR expansion.
.SH "TOML (LIB.TOTOML)"
.PP
Scalars and lists at the top level come first. Attrsets become \fB[tables]\fR, nested ones \fB[a.b]\fR, and lists of attrsets \fB[[arrays of tables]]\fR. An attrset inside a list is written inline. A \fBlib.raw\fR value is written as-is after \fBkey = \fR.
.PP
.EX
{
  terminal.vt = 1;
  default_session = { command = "tuigreet --cmd niri-session"; user = "greeter"; };
}
.EE
.PP
.EX
[default_session]
command = "tuigreet --cmd niri-session"
user = "greeter"

[terminal]
vt = 1
.EE
.SH "RAW"
.PP
The default format. \fBsettings\fR is a string written as-is.
